    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4615-Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-Director-of-Student-Projects-111941-0826/en-GB": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Director of Student Projects) (111941-0826)",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Director of Student Projects) (111941-0826) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Assistant or Associate Professor (Director of Student Projects) (111941-0826) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department School of Engineering Position Type Permanent Salary £47,389 to £56,535 per annum (Assistant Professor) / £58,225 to £67,468 per annum (Associate Professor) About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries about the role, please contact Thomas Popham (Associate Professor) at T.popham@warwick.ac.uk. Flexible Working We will consider applications for employment on a part-time (minimum of 0.8 FTE) or other flexible working basis (e.g. job share), despite the position being advertised as full-time. The School of Engineering is actively seeking a dedicated professional to join as the Director of Student Projects, filling a permanent teaching-focussed Assistant or Associate Professor position. This position represents a unique opportunity to shape the academic landscape by fostering a culture of innovation and skills development through hands-on projects. The Director will lead and manage a portfolio of project modules across our undergraduate and postgraduate taught degree programmes. Working closely with the Head of Education, Directors of Studies and other colleagues, the postholder will coordinate the effective delivery of project modules, ensuring that student projects are aligned with programme learning outcomes, professional accreditation requirements and current developments in engineering practice. This will involve supporting students from project allocation and induction through to supervision, assessment and completion. A key part of the role will be to develop and maintain relationships with industry, professional organisations and other external partners, creating relevant project opportunities that allow students to apply their academic knowledge to complex engineering problems. Through the effective management and continued development of student projects, the post holder will help students build practical, professional and employability skills, which contributes to the School’s reputation for producing graduates with strong academic knowledge and practical engineering expertise. With a growing student body (currently approximately 1600), the Director will play a pivotal role in maintaining and enhancing the overall quality of our educational provision and student experience through project-based learning. This post carries a high administrative load and the Director will be expected to contribute to module teaching on the School’s programmes, plus engage with School activities that may involve evening and weekend work. About You We seek an individual committed to advancing students' skills development through practical applications, ensuring our graduates are well-equipped for success in a dynamic professional landscape. You will have a PhD or equivalent doctoral-level qualification or substantial relevant professional experience in a field directly relevant to the post. A proven track record in delivering high-quality undergraduate teaching, with a focus on innovation and the incorporation of industry-relevant projects into the curriculum, is essential. You will have a good understanding of national policy relating to teaching in higher education, particularly teaching excellence and the mechanisms in place to assess such excellence across institutions. This must include a strong understanding of assessment and curriculum design in the context of undergraduate and taught postgraduate Engineering courses. You must have experience of leading/supervising a variety of projects in a professional and/or academic context, and proven experience in project management. You must be a collegiate colleague who uses their excellent interpersonal, communication and influencing skills to contribute to the School community and work collaboratively with internal and external colleagues and stakeholders. If relevant, you must be willing to complete Warwick’s Academic and Professional Pathway for Teaching Excellence (APP TE) and/or seek (further) HEA accreditation when in post. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department The School of Engineering is a multidisciplinary department that aspires to conduct research to internationally leading standards. The School has recently established Research Clusters, launched in the Summer of 2025. The School of Engineering leads various undergraduate and taught Masters courses and has > 1,600 students on taught courses with an annual Year 1 undergraduate intake of c. 480 students. Students on our undergraduate programmes follow a common programme of study in Year 1. After this, most students choose to join specialised courses. The role of Director of Student Projects was newly introduced approximately two years ago, and provides an opportunity for the successful applicant to shape the School’s portfolio of student projects at undergraduate and postgraduate level. The School holds an Athena Swan Gold award, recognising its sustained commitment to gender equality, inclusion and supportive working practices. Applicants should specify how they can contribute to teaching in their application, giving consideration to both our undergraduate and taught postgraduate programmes. About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply Closing Deadline: Sunday 6 September 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time)* To apply, please click APPLY below and submit an application form by the closing deadline. You will be asked to include a CV (including your full publications list and your employment and education history), and a Cover Letter. Your Cover Letter should: Demonstrate how you meet the essential and desirable criteria in the Job Description using clear examples. Clarify whether you are applying for Assistant or Associate Professor Include a one-page statement demonstrating your teaching expertise and interests Please ensure your application clearly evidences the essential criteria, as the hiring panel may not be able to shortlist your application if these are not demonstrated.** Interview Date: To be confirmed Start Date: To be confirmed * Applications must be submitted through the warwick-careers.tal.net portal before the closing deadline. We recommend completing your application form in good time, as the advert will automatically close at the deadline. ** You are welcome to include any career breaks, such as parental leave, caring responsibilities, or long-term sickness absence. For guidance on how to format a cover letter, see here . What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 30 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. Where possible, we offer hybrid working arrangements that combine office-based and remote working. Opportunities for hybrid working will depend on the requirements of the role, business needs of the department and can be discussed during the recruitment process. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion To find out more about our commitments to equality, diversity, and inclusion, please see: Our Commitments | University of Warwick Job Description JD Assistant or Associate Professor (Director of Student Projects) (111941).pdf – 145KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK and/or are seeking sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa, please follow this link which contains further information about obtaining the right to work in the UK. Cookies Accessibility & Cookie Policy"
    },
    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4533-Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-Climate-Resilient-Infrastructure-and-Transport-Systems-111893-0726/en-GB": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Climate-Resilient Infrastructure and Transport Systems) (111893-0726)",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Climate-Resilient Infrastructure and Transport Systems) (111893-0726) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Assistant or Associate Professor (Climate-Resilient Infrastructure and Transport Systems) (111893-0726) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department School of Engineering Position Type Permanent Salary £47,389 to £56,535 per annum (Assistant Professor) / £58,225 to £67,468 per annum (Associate Professor) About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries about the role, please contact Prof. Xueyu Geng (Professor and Research Cluster Leader for Built Environment and Sustainability) at Xueyu.Geng@warwick.ac.uk. Flexible Working We will consider applications for employment on a part-time or other flexible working basis (e.g. job share), despite the position being advertised as full-time. We welcome applications from candidates seeking a minimum of 0.8 FTE. We invite applications for an Assistant or Associate Professor position in Geoenvironmental Engineering for Low-Carbon, Climate-Resilient Transport Infrastructure Systems, in the Built Environment and Sustainability research cluster within the School of Engineering, University of Warwick. The post is on the Research and Teaching Pathway. The successful applicant will pursue research that makes fundamental contributions to climate adaptation, environmental monitoring, infrastructure resilience, and digital approaches to understanding environmental risk. This includes the application of Earth Observation, satellite-based monitoring, AI-assisted analysis, and geospatial methods to real-world engineering and environmental challenges. Equally important is the integration of these approaches with fundamental experimental and theoretical geomechanics, including advanced laboratory investigations of soil behaviour under changing climatic conditions, fracture and damage processes in geomaterials, and their representation through computational modelling. This integrated experimental–computational–field framework ensures that macro-scale observations are underpinned by rigorous physical understanding. It also presents a significant opportunity for Warwick to develop a distinctive interdisciplinary capability linking civil engineering, geoscience, and digital environmental systems thereby strengthening its position in competitive UKRI and Horizon Europe programmes. Furthermore, it enables strong interdisciplinary collaboration across the University, particularly with Computer Science and Material Science, by integrating engineering geoscience with advanced digital technologies such as AI, machine learning, data analytics, and computer vision alongside experimental geomechanics, to support innovative, data-driven approaches to infrastructure and environmental resilience. This post will contribute to the development of a cohesive research cluster spanning materials development, transport and infrastructure systems, and environmental resilience. This cluster will draw on both experimental and numerical modelling approaches, reinforcing and extending the strengths of existing academic staff. We are especially interested in candidates whose work can leverage or contribute to the School’s collaborative, translational, and industrially engaged research environment. Candidates will be expected to teach to the highest quality on undergraduate, postgraduate and Degree Apprenticeship programmes offered by the School of Engineering, including teaching in Transport Systems, Geospatial Technologies and Environmental Infrastructure Systems. About You We are ideally seeking a candidate with a broad and integrated expertise spanning the full spectrum from fundamental geomechanics to large-scale digital environmental systems. At the micro and meso scales, this includes unsaturated soil mechanics, particle scale behaviour computational modelling to fractures and damages in geomaterials. At the macro scale, this would include geoscience, remote sensing, satellite imaging, GIS, geospatial modelling, AI-enabled environmental analysis, covering infrastructure-related ground systems across all levels of observation and analysis. This would complement and extend Warwick Engineering’s developing strengths in ground engineering, geoenvironmental systems, and computational modelling, while broadening our capabilities into emerging digital and environmental monitoring technologies. You will have a PhD in a relevant discipline. For an Assistant Professor appointment, you must be able to demonstrate the capability to establish an independent research activity, which has the ambition and potential to become internationally leading. If appointed as an Assistant Professor, you will be appointed for a probationary period (usually five years) during which you will be given a reduced teaching and administration load to enable you to establish your research group. Successful completion of probation will coincide with promotion to a permanent Associate Professor position. For an Associate Professor appointment, you will have already established a high-quality independent research activity and have been awarded substantial research funding from external sources, while having experience in successful delivery of teaching activities. At either level, you will initiate, develop and deliver high quality research, publish top-tier journal papers and demonstrate the potential for impact from your research. You will also engage with higher education pedagogy and be willing to develop novel teaching methods, embracing changes in teaching technology and expectations. CEng (or equivalent professional status) is desirable, with the expectation that the successful candidate will be working towards chartership within a defined timeframe (typically 2–3 years). For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department The School of Engineering is a multidisciplinary department that aspires to conduct research to internationally leading standards. The School has recently established Research Clusters, launched in the Summer of 2025. This post aligns to the Built Environment and Sustainability cluster ( https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/eng/engresearch/builtenvironmentandsustainability/ ), which has strong expertise across the fundamental engineering systems that underpin resilient and sustainable infrastructure , and focuses on a broad range of issues underpinning the construction and environmental sectors at both national and international levels. Combining computational modelling with practical experimentation, we tackle issues directly associated with the many challenges posed by the Climate Emergency and linked to UN Sustainable Development Goals. Our research has strong sub-group activities in the fields of Ground Engineering and Structural Engineering. Geotechnical and geoenvironmental research addresses soil behaviour, soil–structure interaction, foundation engineering, and sustainable ground improvement. Research in construction materials encompasses timber engineering, masonry, and innovative low-carbon materials for sustainable construction. These strengths are supported by expertise in computational modelling and digital engineering, enabling the analysis and simulation of complex engineering systems. Additional strengths include structural dynamics, vibration control, earthquake engineering, condition monitoring, infrastructure reliability, and risk assessment, alongside data-driven approaches integrating sensing, machine learning, and optimisation to enhance the performance, durability, and resilience of built infrastructure systems. The Cluster is open to broadening its activities towards geoscience, remote sensing, satellite imaging, GIS, geospatial modelling, AI-enabled environmental analysis, and infrastructure-related ground systems. The School of Engineering leads various undergraduate and taught Masters courses and has > 1,600 students on taught courses with an annual Year 1 undergraduate intake of c. 480 students. Students on our undergraduate programmes follow a common programme of study in Year 1. After this, most students choose to join specialised courses. The Built Environment and Sustainability Cluster is primarily involved in the delivery of the Civil Engineering related UG and PG programmes, including the BEng Degree Apprenticeship in Civil and Infrastructure Engineering and MSc Humanitarian Engineering ( https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/eng/degree_apprenticeships/civil_and_infrastructure_engineering/ ). The School holds an Athena Swan Gold award, recognising its sustained commitment to gender equality, inclusion and supportive working practices. Applicants should specify how they can contribute to teaching in their application, giving consideration to both our undergraduate and taught postgraduate programmes. About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply Closing Deadline: Sunday 6 September 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time)* To apply, please click APPLY below and submit an application form by the closing deadline. You will be asked to include two attachments: The first attachment should be be a curriculum vitae (CV) including your full publications list. The sec ond attachment should be a cover letter. This should (a) state whether you are applying for Assistant or Associate Professor, detailing how you meet each of the essential criteria found in the Job Description document below (desirable criteria too, where possible), (b) include an outline of no more than two pages showing your research vision and plans for grant capture over the next five years; and (c) include a one-page statement demonstrating your teaching expertise and interests. Applicants should specify how they can contribute to teaching in their application, giving consideration to both our undergraduate and taught postgraduate programmes. Attachments other than those requested should not be provided, and applications submitted without the required documents may not be progressed. Applications from candidates in groups underrepresented in the Engineering sector are particularly welcome. The University aims to promote work life balance for all employees and the School of Engineering will consider a range of possible flexible working arrangements in order to recruit the best candidate. Interview Date: To be confirmed Start Date: To be confirmed * Applications must be submitted through the warwick-careers.tal.net portal before the closing deadline. We recommend completing your application form in good time, as the advert will automatically close at the deadline. ** You are welcome to include any career breaks, such as parental leave, caring responsibilities, or long-term sickness absence. For guidance on how to format a cover letter, see here . What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 30 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. Where possible, we offer hybrid working arrangements that combine office-based and remote working. Opportunities for hybrid working will depend on the requirements of the role, business needs of the department and can be discussed during the recruitment process. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion To find out more about our commitments to equality, diversity, and inclusion, please see: Our Commitments | University of Warwick Job Description JD Assistant or Associate Professor (111893).pdf – 123KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK and/or are seeking sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa, please follow this link which contains further information about obtaining the right to work in the UK. Cookies Accessibility & Cookie Policy"
    },
    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4311-Assistant-Professor-Research-Teaching-105617-0626/en-GB": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Research & Teaching) (105617-0626)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Research & Teaching) (105617-0626) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Assistant Professor (Research & Teaching) (105617-0626) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department Warwick Business School Position Type Permanent Salary 'Competitive' About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries, please contact Nicos Nicolaou (Professor of Entrepreneurship and Innovation) at Nicos.Nicolaou@wbs.ac.uk , or Christos Kolympiris (Professor of Entrepreneurship and Innovation) at Christos.Kolympiris@wbs.ac.uk . Flexible Working We will consider applications for employment on a part-time or other flexible working basis (e.g. job share), despite the position being advertised as full-time. Warwick Business School is a research-led, triple-accredited university-based business school with a portfolio of world-class programmes. We are continuing our search for an outstanding scholar to join our faculty. Applications are invited at the level of Assistant Professor in Entrepreneurship and Innovation. The Entrepreneurship and Innovation Group is a world-class group at the heart of a world-class business school. We believe in the power of entrepreneurship to change the world for the better. Our research spans disciplinary boundaries and is published in leading journals. Our exceptional faculty challenge convention and advance new ideas. We embrace diversity, pluralism, and a wide range of perspectives. About You You will have completed or will be near the completion of your doctoral studies, have an outstanding academic record, and show clear promise of developing a top business school career. Evidence of an emerging record of publishing in high quality academic journals would be an advantage. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department Warwick Business School (WBS) is a place where big ideas meet real impact. We’re part of a world-class university, but we’re more than just our reputation. For nearly 60 years, we’ve been helping people turn ambition into action, equipping them to make a real difference in the world. Please view our online brochure , specifically created for candidates, to gain further insight into working at Warwick Business School. About Us We were the first UK business school to achieve triple accreditation (EQUIS, AMBA, AACSB), and our programmes consistently rank among the best - view all of our rankings here . But what really sets us apart is how it feels to be here. We’re down to earth, collaborative, and proud to create a place where staff, students and alumni feel at home. We want your time here to be as inspiring as it is rewarding, with the support, trust, and opportunities you need to grow both professionally and personally. Our Vision The world needs smart ideas, and the leaders who can bring them to life. Those ideas only matter if they can be put into practice. Our vision is simple: to shape the people and ideas that will define what comes next and ensure they have everything they need to make it happen. Everything we do is underpinned by our CORE values and fuelled by a Change Maker mindset. We look for this shared commitment in everyone who joins our community. We nurture that mindset every day by creating a workplace where curiosity is encouraged, ideas are shared openly, and everyone has the chance to turn bold thinking into meaningful action. Change Makers See the bigger picture. They think beyond themselves, seek out different perspectives, and consider the ripple effect of their actions. Challenge the norm. They question the status quo and look for better ways to make a difference. Have the drive to deliver. They don’t just imagine change; they put in the work to make it happen. Our CORE Values Curiosity. We challenge, explore, and create to build a better future. Openness. We welcome ideas, feedback, and change to keep moving forward. Restlessness. We’re never content to stand still; we keep striving for more. Excellence. We hold ourselves to the highest standards in everything we do. About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply CLOSING DEADLINE: Sunday 6 September 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time) To apply, please click APPLY below and submit your application by the closing deadline. You will be asked to include a CV and cover letter. Within these documents, please: 1. Outline your employment and education history (including your most recent employment). 2. Outline the reasons for your interest in this position. 3. Demonstrate how you meet the requirements of the role with clear reference to each of the essential and desirable criteria in the Job Description (PDF attached below). Interview Date: To be confirmed Start Date: August 2027 Applications must be submitted by the closing deadline. Only applications submitted through our Careers Portal will be considered. You are welcome to include any careers breaks you may have taken (e.g. parental/caring/long-term sick leave). Please note that if you do not evidence the essential criteria, the hiring panel may not be able to shortlist your application. For guidance on how to format a cover letter, see here . What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 30 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion Equality, Diversity, & Inclusion Warwick is committed to fostering a diverse, inclusive and respectful community where everyone can thrive. We welcome applications from all backgrounds, cultures, and communities, and actively encourage candidates from underrepresented groups to apply. Find out more about our Social Inclusion work at Warwick . Find out more about our awards and accreditations . We are also one of the six founder institutions of the EUTOPIA European University Alliance . Safeguarding & DBS The University of Warwick is committed to safeguarding and promoting the welfare of all those we work with. Roles involving regulated activity are subject to a Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check at the appropriate level, in line with the DBS Code of Practice. The University will ensure that anyone subject to a barring order does not undertake any work with the barred group (children and/or adults). All employees, volunteers, and partners are expected to share our commitment to safeguarding. Rehabilitation of Ex-Offenders The University will not discriminate against applicants who make a disclosure relating to a conviction. Disclosures at application stage are only visible to the DBS team, not to hiring panels. This ensures fairness and removes bias from the shortlisting process. Job Description JD Assistant Professor (Research & Teaching) (105617).pdf – 121KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK and/or are seeking sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa, please follow this link which contains further information about obtaining the right to work in the UK. Cookies Accessibility & Cookie Policy"
    },
    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4704-Lifeguard-112063-0826/en-GB": {
      "title": "Lifeguard (112063-0826)",
      "description": "Lifeguard (112063-0826) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Lifeguard (112063-0826) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department Warwick Sport Position Type Fixed Term Duration Fixed term contract until 05 October 2027 Salary £25,528 per annum About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries about the role, please contact Daniela Pardal (Commercial Recruitment Consultant) at Daniela.Pardal@warwick.ac.uk Background Checks This role will require an Enhanced DBS Check with Child Barred List. If you are included on a child barred list, you will be breaking the law by applying for this role. Are you passionate about providing excellent levels of customer service, and want to work in an environment where you’ll be supported to grow and develop? If this sounds like you, then we have the perfect role for you. Warwick Sports has an exciting opportunity for a self-motivated and customer orientated individual to join our team as a Lifeguard. Working within the state-of-the-art Sports hub facilities, you’ll work alongside a vibrant team of motivated individuals with one aim – to provide first class customer service, ensuring our customers feel safe and always cared for. As well as your lifeguarding duties half of your role will involve support across the sports hub, from cleaning duties to working closely with Front of House teams with various tasks including equipment setup, event support, resolving customer queries, and increasing our membership base by providing accurate information on products and services. In this role no two days will be the same, you’ll also have the chance to rotate between our different venues supporting the front of house teams, which includes the Sports Hub, Games Hall, Cryfield Pavilion and Tennis Centre, working within different areas of each and supporting duties as directed by Duty Managers. You’ll also be responsible for ensuring all customers that use the facilities including staff members abide by Universities standards and regulations. We continue to actively build an environment where every individual can fulfil their potential and be proud ambassadors for Warwick University, our colleagues, and our customers. You need to be flexible to be able to work shifts with early and late start and finish times,  including weekends and bank holidays. About You If you’re someone with a can-do attitude and would relish the opportunity to work within a highly motivated team who aspire to exceed customers’ expectations every day, whilst helping to improve their physical and mental wellbeing to enable a more active lifestyle then we want to hear from you! For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department The recently approved University strategy for Active Wellness and Sport continues to put students at the heart of their decision making and the creation of this post is the product of strong collaboration with their student body and the commitment to realising their mission. About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply Closing Deadline: Monday 7th September 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time)* To apply, please click HERE and submit an application by the closing deadline. Interview Date: Monday 14th September 2026 Start Date: September/October 2026 What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 26 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. Where possible, we offer hybrid working arrangements that combine office-based and remote working. Opportunities for hybrid working will depend on the requirements of the role, business needs of the department and can be discussed during the recruitment process. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion To find out more about our commitments to equality, diversity, and inclusion, please see: Our Commitments | University of Warwick Job Description JD Lifeguard (112063).pdf – 161KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK We wish to advise applicants that this role is not eligible for sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa. If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK you will need to check the UK Government Website to see if you are eligible to obtain the right to work in the UK through one of the other UK immigration routes before you make your application. Cookies"
    },
    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4587-Front-of-House-Senior-Receptionist-x2-111943-0826/en-GB": {
      "title": "Front of House Senior Receptionist x2 (111943-0826)",
      "description": "Front of House Senior Receptionist x2 (111943-0826) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Front of House Senior Receptionist x2 (111943-0826) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department Warwick Sport Position Type Permanent Salary £26,093 to £26,707 per annum About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries about the role, please contact Sarah Hodson (Assistant Operations Manager – Customer Success) at Sarah.Hodson@warwick.ac.uk. We are looking for a Front of House Senior Receptionist to help lead our reception operation and deliver an exceptional experience for every customer, member, student, staff member and visitor using our facilities. You will play a key role in ensuring a welcoming, safe and professional front-of-house environment while maintaining high standards of customer service, administration, health and safety, and financial compliance. This is an excellent opportunity for a proactive and customer-focused individual with supervisory experience who thrives in a busy environment and is motivated by delivering high-quality service. Key responsibilities In this role, you will: Lead and co-ordinate the day-to-day work of the Reception team to ensure excellent service at all times Provide a welcoming, safe and inclusive environment for all users of Warwick Sport facilities Offer advice, support and responsive handling of customer enquiries Maintain high standards of presentation, cleanliness, health and safety, and compliance across reception areas Carry out routine administrative tasks accurately and efficiently Use systems safely and appropriately, ensuring compliance with GDPR and information security requirements Process financial transactions correctly in line with regulations and assist with stock control Communicate operational and promotional information clearly to customers and colleagues Act as a first aider across sports facilities Support wider Warwick Sport activity, events and initiatives that promote sport and active lifestyles across the University community Work across different Warwick Sport facilities depending on operational requirements About You Warwick Sport is a dynamic and community-focused environment where you can make a real contribution to the experience of students, staff and visitors. You will join a team committed to delivering excellent service, supporting wellbeing and helping people engage with sport and active lifestyles. If you are a confident team leader with a passion for customer experience and operational excellence, we would love to hear from you. Essential skills and experience •    Experience of working in a busy, customer-focused environment •    Excellent customer service skills, with tact and diplomacy •    Strong interpersonal and communication skills •    Confidence using IT systems and databases, including leisure management systems •    The ability to work on your own initiative and motivate others •    Excellent time management and organisational skills •    Excellent written and verbal communication skills For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department Work at one of the UK’s leading universities — Warwick is consistently ranked as a top institution worldwide, with a thriving campus community and reputation for excellence. Warwick Sport plays a vital role in enhancing the student and staff experience at the University of Warwick. We are committed to increasing participation, building communities, supporting active lifestyles, and delivering an outstanding customer experience across our sport and leisure facilities. For further information on the department, please see our webpage here: Gym, fitness, swim & more | Sports and Wellness Hub About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply Closing Deadline: Tuesday 8th September 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time)* To apply, please click HERE and submit an application by the closing deadline. Interview Date: Monday 15th September 2026 Start Date: As soon as possible What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 26 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. Where possible, we offer hybrid working arrangements that combine office-based and remote working. Opportunities for hybrid working will depend on the requirements of the role, business needs of the department and can be discussed during the recruitment process. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion To find out more about our commitments to equality, diversity, and inclusion, please see: Our Commitments | University of Warwick Job Description JD Front of House Senior Receptionist (111943).pdf – 142KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK We wish to advise applicants that this role is not eligible for sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa. If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK you will need to check the UK Government Website to see if you are eligible to obtain the right to work in the UK through one of the other UK immigration routes before you make your application. Cookies"
    },
    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4700-Warwick-Zeeman-Lecturer-x2-102592-0826/en-GB": {
      "title": "Warwick Zeeman Lecturer x2 (102592-0826)",
      "description": "Warwick Zeeman Lecturer x2 (102592-0826) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Warwick Zeeman Lecturer x2 (102592-0826) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department Mathematics Position Type Fixed Term Duration Fixed term contract for 3 years. Salary £47,389 to £56,535 per annum About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries about the role, please contact Professor James Robinson (Head of Department, Mathematics) at J.C.Robinson@warwick.ac.uk Or Professor Marie-Therese Wolfram (Co-chair of EDI Committee) at M.Wolfram@warwick.ac.uk or Dr Bryn Davies (Chair of Early Career Committee) at Bryn.Davies@warwick.ac.uk. The Warwick Mathematics Institute invites applications for the post of Warwick Zeeman Lecturer (WZL), to start in September 2027. We have two positions available, open to all areas of pure and applied mathematics. WZLs are temporary appointments for a fixed term of three years. They offer the opportunity to join an excellent mathematics department, with plenty of time to pursue independent research while gaining the experience in lecturing and administration that will strengthen any future application for a permanent academic position. About You All applicants should hold a PhD and have already demonstrated excellence in research and an ability to teach effectively. We are seeking applicants who can make a strong contribution to both our research and our teaching, and who will make the most of the opportunities provided by our lively and vibrant departmental culture. In the past, many WZLs have extended the term of their appointment by combining this post with a Leverhulme Early Career Fellowship and this gives a good indication of the kind of career stage and level of independence we expect for this position. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department The Warwick Mathematics Institute is internationally recognised for its research and our best undergraduates are a match for those at any other UK university. We are one of only five UK mathematics departments in the world top 30* and we are the 5th highest UK mathematics department in the Complete University Guide**. We run a wide range of weekly seminar series, and many workshops and conferences, for which our Mathematics Research Centre provides organisational support. A list of our staff and their research interests can be found at https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/maths/research/researchers and you can see our undergraduate module pages at https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/maths/currentstudents/ughandbook The department encourages the career advancement and promotion of all its staff. All Zeeman Lecturers have a mentor and a more formal, supportive, annual review of their progress. We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment, and we actively encourage applications from groups that are under-represented in UK mathematics. We are working hard towards improving our gender balance: we currently hold a Bronze Athena Swan award and our energetic Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion committee plays a significant role in shaping departmental policy. We are an internationally diverse department. For those eligible, the University offers relocation and Immigration Support Scheme packages to assist with costs of relocating and any associated visa applications and HIS (health) surcharge fees. *QS World Rankings University by Subject 2026: Mathematics ** Complete University Guide Subject Rankings League Table, Mathematics, 2026 About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply Closing Deadline: Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time)* Please read the following carefully before submitting an application. You can find more details about what we expect in your application documents on the department’s vacancies page. Applications should include: a covering letter and research statement combined as one document a full CV. Your covering letter (no longer than two pages) should start with a brief indication of your research field in order to help us identify your subject area quickly (for example “Probability: percolation and other lattice models”; “Machine Learning: mathematical foundations). It should also include a brief summary of your research and its impact for a non-specialist audience. You may also use your letter to highlight any aspects of your experience that are particularly relevant to the selection criteria, but you need not repeat information already included in your CV. We value and respect diverse gender identities and expressions, and encourage applicants who wish to do so to share their preferred personal pronouns (e.g. he/him, she/her, they/them) with us in this letter too. Your research statement should be no longer than 4 pages of 11pt text. Here you should summarise your research achievements to date and how you plan to develop your research programme at Warwick. You may wish to comment on how the Warwick environment, including potential collaborators and other resources, would support your plans. Your CV should contain a full list of your publications, any grants or prizes that you have been awarded, and a summary of your teaching experience. Because terminology varies between institutions and countries, please indicate briefly the responsibilities associated with any teaching positions you have held. We do not ask for a longer teaching statement. We encourage applicants to include in their CV details of any career breaks or other circumstances that may have affected their research, teaching or professional activities. An appointments panel of around 12 members of academic staff representing a broad range of subject areas in the department are responsible for choosing candidates to invite to interview. Typically the panel meets twice: once to produce a longlist, and once to decide which candidates to invite to interview. Your application documents will be made available to all permanent academic staff in the department, whose comments are used to inform the considerations of the appointments panel. You will need to provide the names and contact details of three referees as part of your application. These will need to be entered on the application webform and should not appear elsewhere in your application . We will request references directly from referees for all longlisted candidates and these will be used to assist the panel’s decisions on who to invite for interview. We currently expect to make this longlisting decision on 16th November, and will hope to receive all references by 23rd November, so please make sure your referees are aware of this timescale. Those selected for interview will be invited to the department to give a talk on their research on Monday 25th January 2027, followed by a formal interview on Tuesday 26th January 2027. These dates have already been fixed to ensure the availability of the Chair of Faculty and cannot be changed. Interview Date: 25th and 26th January 2027 (attendance required both days) Start Date: September 2027 * Applications must be submitted through the warwick-careers.tal.net portal before the closing deadline. We recommend completing your application form in good time, as the advert will automatically close at the deadline. What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 30 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. Where possible, we offer hybrid working arrangements that combine office-based and remote working. Opportunities for hybrid working will depend on the requirements of the role, business needs of the department and can be discussed during the recruitment process. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion To find out more about our commitments to equality, diversity, and inclusion, please see: Our Commitments | University of Warwick Job Description JD - Warwick Zeeman Lecturer (102592, 104517).pdf – 92KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK and/or are seeking sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa, please follow this link which contains further information about obtaining the right to work in the UK. Cookies Accessibility & Cookie Policy"
    },
    "https://warwick-careers.tal.net/vx/lang-en-GB/mobile-0/appcentre-1/brand-4/user-11595/xf-0a29ed012b2b/candidate/so/pm/1/pl/3/opp/4699-Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-x2-112030-0826/en-GB": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor x2 (112030-0826)",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor x2 (112030-0826) - University of Warwick By clicking \"I accept\" you consent to the use of cookies on our website to support technical features and anonymous statistics I accept I accept the cookie policy Opt Out I would like to opt out of cookies Read more Read more about our cookie policy Skip to content (Opens in a new window) Login Create Account HOME OPPORTUNITIES HELP CENTRE Login Create Account Assistant or Associate Professor x2 (112030-0826) Location University of Warwick Campus, Coventry Department Mathematics Position Type Permanent Salary £47,389 to £56,535 per annum (Assistant Professor) / £58,225 to £67,468 per annum (Associate Professor) About the Role Informal Queries For informal queries about the role, please contact Professor James Robinson (Head of Department, Mathematics) at J.C.Robinson@warwick.ac.uk or Professor Marie-Therese Wolfram (Co-chair of EDI Committee) at M.Wolfram@warwick.ac.uk or Dr Bryn Davies (Chair of Early Career Committee) at Bryn.Davies@warwick.ac.uk. 2 x Assistant/Associate Professors Assistant Professor £47,389 – £56,535 Associate Professor £58,255 - £67,468 The Warwick Mathematics Institute invites applications for the post of Assistant or Associate Professor, to start in September 2027. We are open to applications in all areas of pure and applied mathematics. Associate Professorships are permanent appointments and usually for those who already have a well-established track record in research and significant lecturing experience. Those appointed at the Assistant level are supported through a five-year probationary period to enable them to fulfil the criteria for promotion to Associate Professor. About You All applicants should hold a PhD and have already demonstrated both excellence and independence in research along with an ability to teach effectively. We are seeking candidates who can make a strong contribution to both our research and our teaching, and who will make the most of the opportunities provided by our lively and vibrant departmental culture. For details on the experience and skills required, please refer to the job description attached as a PDF below. About the Department The Warwick Mathematics Institute is internationally recognised for its research and our best undergraduates are a match for those at any other UK university. We are one of only five UK mathematics departments in the world top 30* and we are the 5th highest UK mathematics department in the Complete University Guide**. We run a wide range of weekly seminar series, and many workshops and conferences, for which our Mathematics Research Centre provides organisational support. A list of our staff and their research interests can be found at https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/maths/research/researchers and you can see our undergraduate module pages at https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/sci/maths/currentstudents/ughandbook The department encourages the career advancement and promotion of all its staff, supporting promotion from Associate Professor to Reader and subsequently Full Professor in the same way that Assistant Professors are guided through the probationary process on their way to becoming Associate Professors. We are committed to fostering an inclusive environment, and we would particularly welcome applications from groups that are under-represented in UK mathematics. We are working hard towards improving our gender balance: we currently hold a Bronze Athena Swan award and our energetic Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion committee plays a significant role in shaping departmental policy. We are an internationally diverse department. For those eligible, the University offers relocation and Immigration Support Scheme packages to assist with costs of relocating and any associated visa applications and HIS (health) surcharge fees. *QS World Rankings University by Subject 2026: Mathematics **Complete University Guide Subject Rankings League Table, Mathematics, 2026 About the University We are a world-leading research-intensive university founded in 1965. We are ranked 74th in the world and 9th in the UK. * Additionally, 92% of our research is rated world-leading or internationally excellent .** Find out more about us at warwick.ac.uk/about/ . * World University Ranking 2026 , Complete University Guide 2026 ** Research Excellence Framework 2021 How to Apply Closing Deadline: Sunday 18 October 2026 at 11:55pm (UK Time)* Please read the following carefully before submitting an application. You can find more details about what we expect in your application documents on the department’s vacancies page. Applications should include: a covering letter, a research statement, and a full CV. Your covering letter (no longer than two pages) should start with a brief indication of your research field in order to help us identify your subject area quickly (for example “Probability: percolation and other lattice models”; “Machine Learning: mathematical foundations”). It should also include a brief summary of your research and its impact for a non-specialist audience. You may also use your letter to highlight any aspects of your experience that are particularly relevant to the selection criteria, but you need not repeat information already included in your CV. If you would only consider an Associate Professorship, please make this clear in your covering letter. We value and respect diverse gender identities and expressions, and encourage applicants who wish to do so to share their preferred personal pronouns (e.g. he/him, she/her, they/them) with us in this letter too. Your research statement should be no longer than 4 pages of 11pt text (including any references). Here you should summarise your research achievements to date and how you plan to develop your research programme at Warwick. You may wish to comment on how the Warwick environment, including potential collaborators and other resources, would support your plans. Your CV should contain a full list of your publications, any grants or prizes that you have been awarded, and a summary of your teaching experience. Because terminology varies between institutions and countries, please indicate briefly the responsibilities associated with any teaching positions you have held. We do not ask for a longer teaching statement. We encourage applicants to include in their CV details of any career breaks or other circumstances that may have affected their research, teaching or professional activities. An appointments panel of around 12 members of academic staff representing a broad range of subject areas in the department are responsible for choosing candidates to invite to interview. Typically the panel meet twice: once to produce a longlist, and once to decide which candidates to invite to interview. Your application documents will be made available to all permanent academic staff in the department, whose comments are used to inform the considerations of the appointments panel. You will need to provide the names and contact details of three referees as part of your application. These will need to be entered on the application webform and should not appear elsewhere in your application. We will request references directly from referees for all longlisted candidates and these will be used to assist the panel’s decisions on who to invite for interview. We currently expect to make this longlisting decision on 9th November, and will hope to receive all references by 16th November, so please make sure your referees are aware of this timescale. Those selected for interview will be invited to the department to give a talk on their research on Monday 18th January 2027, followed by a formal interview on Tuesday 19th January 2027. These dates have already been fixed to ensure the availability of the Chair of Faculty and cannot be changed. Interview Date: January 18th 2027 and January 19th 2027 (candidates required both days) Start Date: September 2027 * Applications must be submitted through the warwick-careers.tal.net portal before the closing deadline. We recommend completing your application form in good time, as the advert will automatically close at the deadline. What we Offer We provide a comprehensive range of benefits, including: An attractive pension scheme. 30 days holiday plus University Christmas closure. Generous parental/adoption leave policy. Onsite childcare facilities. Excellent learning and development opportunities. We recognise the importance of a healthy work/life balance and offer access to flexible working arrangements. Where possible, we offer hybrid working arrangements that combine office-based and remote working. Opportunities for hybrid working will depend on the requirements of the role, business needs of the department and can be discussed during the recruitment process. For more information, see here . We are proud to be a Living Wage employer . Our Commitment to Inclusion To find out more about our commitments to equality, diversity, and inclusion, please see: Our Commitments | University of Warwick Job Description JD - Assistant or Associate Professor (112030, 112031).pdf – 118KB Opens in a new window Right to Work in the UK If you do not yet have the right to work in the UK and/or are seeking sponsorship for a Skilled Worker visa, please follow this link which contains further information about obtaining the right to work in the UK. Cookies Accessibility & Cookie Policy"
    }
  },
